    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4258",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246608/assets/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$",
